  "interpretation": "Within a dataset -- user and item counts fixed, only the number of observations varying, which is the comparison the claim is about -- cost grows at most linearly in N. Pooling across datasets raises the fitted exponent above 1, but that reflects a constant vertical offset rather than superlinear growth: the momentum update is dense over all factor rows, so each step carries a cost proportional to n_users + n_items on top of the gradient, and the larger dataset pays more of it at equal N."
